Looking at this problem, I need to set up coordinates, compute the first iteration, identify the pattern, and sum the resulting geometric series.

## Setup

Place $B_1=(0,0)$, $C_1=(14,0)$. From $A_1B_1=16$, $A_1C_1=10$:
$$A_1 = \left(\tfrac{88}{7},\, \tfrac{40\sqrt{3}}{7}\right)$$

Check angle: $\cos A_1 = \frac{10^2+16^2-14^2}{2\cdot 10\cdot 16} = \frac{160}{320}=\frac12$, so $A_1=60°$.

Area $[A_1B_1C_1] = \frac12\cdot 10\cdot 16\cdot\frac{\sqrt3}{2}=40\sqrt3$.

**Circumcenter** $O_1$: lies on $x=7$ (perp. bisector of $B_1C_1$). Solving $|O_1B_1|=|O_1A_1|$ gives $O_1=(7,\, \frac{7\sqrt3}{3})$, with circumradius $R=\frac{14}{\sqrt3}$.

## Computing $A_2, B_2, C_2$

**$A_2$**: On $B_1C_1$ with $C_1A_2=2\,B_1A_2$, so $B_1A_2=\frac{14}{3}$: $\;A_2=(\frac{14}{3},0)$.

**$B_2$**: Intersection of line $A_1C_1$ with circumcircle of $\{O_1,A_2,C_1\}$, other than $C_1$.

Since $A_2,C_1$ lie on the $x$-axis, the circumcircle center is on $x=\frac{28}{3}$. Solving for equal distances to $O_1$ and $A_2$ yields center $(\frac{28}{3},0)$, radius $\frac{14}{3}$ (verified: $O_1$ lies on it).

Parametrize line $A_1C_1$: $P=(14,0)+t(-\frac{10}{7},\frac{40\sqrt3}{7})$. Substituting into the circle:
$$\left(\tfrac{14}{3}-\tfrac{10t}{7}\right)^2+\tfrac{4800t^2}{49}=\tfrac{196}{9}$$
Expanding and simplifying: $t\!\left(-\tfrac{280}{21}+100t\right)=0$, so $t=0$ (gives $C_1$) or $t=\frac{2}{15}$.

$$B_2 = \left(\tfrac{290}{21},\, \tfrac{16\sqrt3}{21}\right)$$

**$C_2$**: Intersection of line $A_1B_1$ with circumcircle of $\{O_1,A_2,B_1\}$, other than $B_1$.

Center on $x=\frac{7}{3}$; solving gives center $(\frac{7}{3},\frac{7\sqrt3}{3})$, radius $\frac{14}{3}$ (verified).

Parametrize line $A_1B_1$: $P=s(\frac{88}{7},\frac{40\sqrt3}{7})$. Substituting:
$$\tfrac{(264s-49)^2+3(120s-49)^2}{441}=\tfrac{196}{9}$$
This gives $s(112896s-61152)=0$, so $s=\frac{13}{24}$.

$$C_2 = \left(\tfrac{143}{21},\, \tfrac{65\sqrt3}{21}\right)$$

## Side lengths of $A_2B_2C_2$

$$|A_2B_2|^2=\left(\tfrac{64}{7}\right)^2+\left(\tfrac{16\sqrt3}{21}\right)^2=\tfrac{12544}{147}=\tfrac{256}{3}\implies |A_2B_2|=\tfrac{16}{\sqrt3}$$
$$|B_2C_2|^2=7^2+\left(\tfrac{7\sqrt3}{3}\right)^2=\tfrac{196}{3}\implies |B_2C_2|=\tfrac{14}{\sqrt3}$$
$$|C_2A_2|^2=\left(\tfrac{15}{7}\right)^2+\left(\tfrac{65\sqrt3}{21}\right)^2=\tfrac{4900}{147}=\tfrac{100}{3}\implies |C_2A_2|=\tfrac{10}{\sqrt3}$$

The sides are exactly $\frac{1}{\sqrt3}$ times $(16,14,10)$ — **$A_2B_2C_2$ is similar to $A_1B_1C_1$ with ratio $\frac{1}{\sqrt3}$**, with the same vertex correspondence.

**Direct area check**: $[A_2B_2C_2]=\frac{\sqrt3}{882}|{-4802+18850-2288}|=\frac{11760\sqrt3}{882}=\frac{40\sqrt3}{3}$. ✓

## Self-similarity → Geometric series

The construction depends only on the triangle's *shape* (angles and side ratios), not its size. Since $A_2B_2C_2\sim A_1B_1C_1$ with identical vertex roles, applying the same construction yields $A_3B_3C_3\sim A_2B_2C_2$ with the same ratio $\frac{1}{\sqrt3}$, and so on inductively.

The areas form a geometric series with common ratio $\frac{1}{3}$:
$$[A_iB_iC_i] = 40\sqrt3\cdot\left(\tfrac{1}{3}\right)^{i-1}$$

## Final computation

$$\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}[A_iB_iC_i]=40\sqrt3\cdot\frac{1}{1-\frac13}=40\sqrt3\cdot\frac32=60\sqrt3$$

$$\left(\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}[A_iB_iC_i]\right)^2=(60\sqrt3)^2=3600\cdot 3=10800$$

